<DocsLayout
  title="TUI"
  eyebrow="Surfaces"
  description="The Ratatui terminal client for the Roder app server."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/goal-mode/", title: "Plan mode & goals" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/chrome/", title: "Chrome browser control" }}
>
  <p>
    <code>roder-tui</code> is the reference terminal client. It uses
    <a href="https://github.com/ratatui-org/ratatui" rel="noopener">Ratatui</a> and
    <code>crossterm</code>, but it talks to the runtime through the same local app-client
    path a desktop or automation client can use.
  </p>

  <h2>Model switching and failed follow-ups</h2>
  <p>
    The model picker avoids loading the full conversation history. Switching from
    SuperGrok to OpenAI or Codex handles oversized history identifiers and incompatible
    reasoning fields during replay. If a follow-up request is rejected, the TUI shows
    the failure and keeps the input for <code>/retry</code>, so you can resolve the cause
    and resend it without reconstructing the prompt.
  </p>

  <h2>What it shows</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Streaming assistant output and tool-call timeline.</li>
    <li>Approval prompts and policy-mode state.</li>
    <li>Provider, model, session, command, policy, and agent-mode choices through the command palette and composer title.</li>
    <li>Multiline composer input with Shift+Enter, plus Ctrl+J and Alt+Enter fallbacks when the terminal cannot report modified Enter keys.</li>
    <li><code>/agent-swarm</code> and <code>/swarm</code> commands, live child-count progress, and compact ordered result grids for parallel agent work.</li>
    <li>Composer mention pickers for workspace files with <code>@</code> and skills with <code>$</code>.</li>
    <li>Knowledge browsing through <code>/knowledge</code> commands and command-palette entries.</li>
    <li>Media generation palette entries for image and video prompts, with artifact rows in the timeline.</li>
    <li>Remote-node authority banners when the TUI is controlling a full runtime on another machine.</li>
    <li>Diff previews for file changes with keyboard-driven accept/reject handling.</li>
    <li>Dynamic workflow trigger hints, approval previews, and run progress below the composer.</li>
    <li>Shell and exec sessions as one timeline entry, with streamed stdin updates folded into the open detail view.</li>
    <li>Native terminal progress indicators while turns run, pause for approval, or fail.</li>
    <li>Roadmap orchestration panels for plan rows, task status, worker activity, and validation evidence.</li>
    <li>Status segments contributed by built-ins and extensions.</li>
    <li>Mouse selection, scrolling, hover, and clickable timeline interactions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is a client</h2>
  <p>
    The TUI should not be the only owner of runtime state. Keeping it as a client of
    <code>roder-app-server</code> means terminal, desktop, test harness, and SDK
    surfaces can share protocol methods and event semantics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Agent-swarm mode follows that rule: the TUI toggles per-thread runtime state and
    renders app-server events rather than owning a separate client-only mode. Entering
    swarm from a gating policy asks for the required policy switch before work starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Composer input</h2>
  <p>
    Press Enter to submit the current prompt. Press Shift+Enter to insert a newline
    while staying in the composer, which is useful for multi-paragraph instructions,
    pasted checklists, or context blocks. In terminals that cannot distinguish
    Shift+Enter from Enter, use Ctrl+J or Alt+Enter for a newline.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the TUI is running inside tmux, Roder enables CSI-u modified-key reporting for
    the active pane when needed so Ghostty and other compatible terminals can send
    Shift+Enter as a distinct key event.
  </p>
  <h2>Recover queued and steered prompts</h2>
  <p>
    The TUI retains submitted text and images until admission is known. Rejected
    input stays available to <code>/retry</code> in submission order. Use
    <code>/retry discard</code> to drop only the oldest retained input in the
    current thread without clearing later follow-ups.
  </p>
  <p>
    A timeout or lost response does not prove rejection. Roder does not
    automatically resend uncertain input: inspect thread history before retrying.
    Queues pause for active turns, settings or approval dialogs, plan-exit decisions,
    and source-copy mode. This queue is TUI-session state, not a durable offline
    outbox; closing the app cannot recover prompts the backend never accepted.
  </p>
  <h2>Read Markdown tables</h2>
  <p>
    Since CLI 0.4.4, assistant Markdown pipe tables render as aligned grids with a
    header rule instead of raw pipe-delimited rows. Tables that fit the transcript
    keep their columns aligned. When a table is too wide, the TUI switches to labeled
    rows so wrapping does not mix neighboring columns. You do not need to widen the
    terminal to separate the values.
  </p>
  <p>
    This changes the rendered transcript, not the response source:
    <code>/copy</code> still copies the original Markdown.
  </p>
  <h2>Copy original Markdown or code</h2>
  <p>
    Run <code>/copy</code> to select the latest assistant response or a fenced code
    block. Use j/k, arrow keys, or Tab/Shift+Tab to move, Home/End for the first or
    last target, and PageUp/PageDown to scroll the preview. Enter or y copies;
    Escape, q, or Ctrl+C closes the dialog.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whole-response copies include original Markdown. Code copies omit fences and
    preserve tabs, whitespace, line endings, and missing final newlines. Terminal
    wrapping and image placements never become clipboard text. Clipboard failures
    leave the selection available for retry.
  </p>
  <h2>Links and inline image previews</h2>
  <p>
    Markdown links display their labels with clickable destinations. Supported
    Kitty/Sixel terminals can preview local attached, viewed, and generated images
    inline. Unsupported terminals or invalid images fall back to metadata and paths.
    Decoding and rendering are bounded; overlays suspend image placements.
    Remote or unknown paths are not treated as files on the host.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/master/docs/prompt-admission.md">prompt recovery rules</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/master/docs/tui-source-copy.md">source-copy controls</a>
    for details. Prompt recovery, source copy, and inline previews shipped in CLI 0.4.0.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
